      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What if I run out of words for a letter?</summary>
          <p>Never strain to find a difficult word. If you cannot think of a word starting with a letter within five seconds, simply advance to the next letter in your seed word or choose a new seed word entirely.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can I visualize people or familiar memories?</summary>
          <p>It is best to avoid visualizing familiar people, work colleagues, or emotionally charged memories. Stick to mundane, concrete physical objects (fruits, furniture, landscapes, household items) to avoid triggering emotional associations.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How long does it take for Cognitive Shuffling to induce sleep?</summary>
          <p>In observational studies and user reports, most people experience deep drowsiness within 8 to 15 minutes of continuous practice. Often, individuals drift off mid-visualization without realizing they have transitioned into sleep.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does Cognitive Shuffling work for daytime power naps?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Because it quickly bypasses active prefrontal cortex loops, many people use Cognitive Shuffling to fall asleep rapidly for 20-minute afternoon naps without feeling restless.</p>
        </details>
